  <!-- 05 Reflection and next steps -->
  <section id="next" class="chapter" aria-labelledby="next-title">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="chapter-line"><span class="chapter-n" aria-hidden="true">05</span><p class="chapter-name" aria-hidden="true">Looking back</p><span class="chapter-rule" aria-hidden="true"></span></div>
      <h2 id="next-title" class="claim"><span class="visually-hidden">Looking back: </span>I wish I’d joined earlier, and brought more Red Bull.</h2>
      <div class="prose reflection">
        <p>This is one of the most impactful projects I’ve worked on. Moving a process from pen and paper to digital is a real quality-of-life improvement for everyone involved at Sam’s, from associates on the floor to the asset protection and operations managers who oversee them.</p>
        <p>I underestimated how much knowledge of the old process would shape the new one. Years of paper habits decided what managers expected from the dashboard, the filters and the approval. Designing the flow also meant designing for that change, especially for longer-tenured associates.</p>
        <p>If I could do it again, I’d get involved earlier. I’d go to more inventory nights and talk with more MAPMs and associates up front about what would help them. A lot of what we discovered deep into the project could have surfaced in the first few weeks.</p>
      </div>
      <h3 class="sub">What testing told us to do next</h3>
      <ol class="next-steps">
        <li><strong>Map the 3rd party’s tags to club locations.</strong> Managers called it a huge win that would make counting much more efficient.</li>
        <li><strong>Let managers undo or edit an approval.</strong> Something can come up later in the night, and an undo would take the pressure off.</li>
        <li><strong>Mark the finish.</strong> Managers wanted a clear “you’re done” screen instead of wondering if there was more to do.</li>
        <li><strong>Scan to enter a location.</strong> Associates tried to scan the aisle barcode to start a count, so let them.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

